Beryllium: least Capsicum rights on descriptors a confined process creates or receives

Purpose: Phase 2 increment 3 (plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md; feature matrix
  S-REN-04, S-REN-05, S-IPC-01). The renderer lockdown
  (beryllium-capsicum-renderer.patch) cuts every descriptor that exists at
  the lockdown point to the rights its kind needs. The owner's runs of
  0.20.3.8 (7 November 2026, with security.bsd.unprivileged_proc_debug=1)
  showed what that leaves: everything a renderer creates or receives after
  the lockdown has every right. Per renderer that was a kqueue and an
  eventfd for each thread that starts a message pump after the lockdown
  (the IO thread, the compositor and others), every socket the browser
  sends for a transport to another process, and the font files the font
  service passes. Capsicum has no default rights for new descriptors, so
  each must be cut where it appears. This patch adds, on FreeBSD only:
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: the policy in one place. A kind per
    descriptor (read-only file, socket, kqueue, eventfd or pipe, shared
    memory, directory) with its rights, fcntl commands and ioctls, taken
    as the intersection with what the descriptor has (rights only ever
    shrink, so the kernel never refuses); the kind from a kinfo_file entry
    (for the lockdown sweep, outside capability mode) and from fstat for a
    process in capability mode, where kern.proc.filedesc and F_KINFO are
    refused: an empty kevent answers only on a kqueue and F_GET_SEALS only
    on shared memory, and neither needs a right. The lockdown patch now
    uses this instead of its own copy.
  - base/message_loop/message_pump_epoll.cc: each pump cuts its epoll
    descriptor (epoll-shim's kqueue on FreeBSD: kevent, event, fstat) and
    its wake eventfd at creation, in every process. The survey of 0.20.3.8
    found 6 million refusals after the lockdown because the kqueue policy
    lacked CAP_EVENT while epoll-shim's epoll_wait() polls its kqueue
    before each kevent(); the policy here includes it.
  - mojo/public/cpp/platform/socket_utils_posix.cc: a process in
    capability mode cuts every descriptor it receives over a Mojo channel
    (SocketRecvmsg serves both the ipcz transports and the broker client)
    to its kind's rights, and removes every right from one the policy has
    no kind for (a writable file, a device...), logging what it was; the
    descriptor stays, so whoever expected it gets one that can do nothing
    rather than a missing handle.
  This is defence in depth for a renderer that is not compromised, and it
  makes the kernel's view match the policy (procstat -C, the suite's
  policy test). It is not the security boundary: a compromised renderer
  can call recvmsg itself. The boundary is the browser limiting what it
  sends (S-IPC-01), the next increment, which needs to know which child a
  transport leads to.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). beryllium-capsicum-renderer.patch depends on
  this one (the helper); applies after the imported FreeBSD patches
  patch-base_BUILD.gn and patch-base_message__loop_message__pump__epoll.cc
  (stage 2).
  Sockets (7 November 2026, after the adversarial review, plan/16 BER-02):
  a socket is admitted only in the shape of a Mojo transport, an end of an
  unnamed AF_UNIX stream or seqpacket pair, not listening, bound or
  connected to no path (kinfo_file kf_sock_domain, kf_sock_type,
  kf_sa_local, kf_sa_peer at the lockdown; SO_DOMAIN, SO_TYPE,
  SO_ACCEPTCONN, getsockname, getpeername for a received one). A network
  socket or one connected to a named service keeps authority that rights
  cannot take back, so it ends the lockdown or loses every right.
  ExpectConfinement() (0.20.3.11): the lockdown calls it before the table
  snapshot, so the receive paths limit from that moment rather than from
  capability mode, closing any window between the snapshot and cap_enter()
  for a thread that might run meanwhile. (It was written in the belief that
  the IO thread was already receiving Mojo messages at the lockdown; it is
  not: RendererMain() locks down before RenderThreadImpl exists, and the
  two unlimited sockets of 0.20.3.10 were pairs the renderer made itself,
  below. The precaution stays.) The same limit also covers
  base::UnixDomainSocket::RecvMsgWithFlags (the sandbox IPC replies that
  carry font files), the other recvmsg path in the renderer.
  Sockets a confined process creates (0.20.3.11, 8 November 2026): with the
  receive window covered, each confined renderer still held two sockets
  with every right, both unix stream, one blocking and one not, with the
  browser at the other end, and the log showed nothing refused: they were
  made in the renderer with socketpair() and one end sent away. A new
  socket has every right, so LimitCreatedSocketPair() cuts both ends of
  every pair a confined process makes to a transport's rights, at the
  three places Chromium makes them: base::CreateSocketPair (the sandbox
  IPC request pairs), base::SyncSocket::CreatePair and
  mojo::PlatformChannel, and logs the pair with the site and the thread that
  made it (0.20.3.12: one pair on CrRendererMain right after the lockdown,
  one on Compositor, and the survey of 0.20.3.11 showed one such blocking
  socket to the browser in every child process type, so this is Chromium's
  pattern; the site in the log, from 0.20.3.13, names it). 0.20.3.13
  (9 November 2026) named it: mojo::PlatformChannel on both CrRendererMain
  and Compositor, one pair each per renderer, all cut at creation. Which
  caller opens them is still to be named: from 0.20.3.14 the first pair
  made at each site on each thread is logged with a stack trace (the
  package keeps function names), once per process.
  The sender's side (9 November 2026, build 0.20.3.13; plan/14 increment 5,
  S-IPC-01): the feature kBerylliumCapsicumRenderer moves here from
  sandbox_freebsd.cc so the browser can read it too; ExpectConfinedPeer(),
  ForgetConfinedPeer() and IsConfinedPeer() keep the pids of the renderers
  the browser launched with the lockdown on; LimitSentDescriptor() cuts a
  descriptor bound for one of them to its kind's rights before sendmsg()
  (beryllium-sender-side-rights.patch calls it from Mojo's transport and
  registers the pids from the launcher). A new kind, kWritableFile (pread,
  pwrite, ftruncate, fsync, read-write mapping, fstat, F_GETFL), is what a
  file the browser hands a renderer on purpose (a File System Access
  handle) keeps; the receive path admits it with those rights instead of
  removing every right, while the lockdown point still refuses any
  writable file. 0.20.3.13 ran it (9 November 2026): the browser cut 1 to
  88 descriptors per renderer, none refused outright, and the policy held
  on every renderer read. One effect on the receive path: a socket the
  browser has cut arrives without CAP_GETSOCKNAME and CAP_GETPEERNAME, so
  the receive path's getsockname() and getpeername() were refused, 242
  times in 20 renderers in the 0.20.3.13 survey (two per received socket;
  kdump printed the needed right as "invalid cap_rights_t", because on
  this CURRENT the kernel's constant rights sets, CAP_RIGHTS_INITIALIZER,
  leave the index bits of their unused word unset and libsysdecode
  rejects the set instead of naming the right). From 0.20.3.14 the
  receive path asks a socket's names only when it holds the rights to ask
  (cap_rights_get), and takes a socket its sender cut as unnamed and
  unconnected, which the sender checked with the rights it had.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. The
  0.20.3.13 state compiled and ran (9 November 2026); the rights check
  before getsockname and the stack trace are for build 0.20.3.14, not yet
  compiled.
